### 6.5 FMCG / Consumer Staples

**Sector profile:** `FMCG`
**Critical insight:** Volume growth matters more than value growth; valuation is always optically high — compare to own history, not market.

**Fundamental Score:**

```
Fundamental_FMCG = 
    (Growth_Quality × 0.28) +
    (Profitability × 0.23) +
    (Capital_Efficiency × 0.20) +
    (Brand_Strength × 0.15) +
    (Governance × 0.05) +
    (Valuation × 0.09)
```

#### Growth Quality (volume-weighted)

```
Growth_Quality = 
    (Volume_Growth_Score × 0.60) +
    (Value_Growth_Score × 0.30) +
    (Rural_Mix_Score × 0.10)

Volume_Growth_Score = HIB(Vol Growth%, floor=0, ceiling=10)
```

#### Capital Efficiency (FMCG should be capital-light)

```
Capital_Efficiency = 
    (ROCE_Score × 0.50) +
    (ROE_Score × 0.30) +
    (Working_Capital_Days_Score × 0.20)

ROCE_Score              = HIB(ROCE%, floor=20, ceiling=50)
Working_Capital_Days    = LIB(WC days, floor=10, ceiling=60) [sector percentile]
```

#### Brand Strength (proxy: pricing power)

```
Brand_Strength = 
    (Gross_Margin_Stability_Score × 0.50) +
    (A&P_Intensity_Score × 0.30) +
    (Distribution_Reach_Score × 0.20)

Gross_Margin_Stability:
    100 if 5Y std-dev of GM% < 1.5
    0  if > 5
A&P_Intensity:
    Optimal range — too low = under-invested brand, too high = competitive pressure
```

#### Valuation (relative to OWN history only)

```
Valuation_FMCG = 100 − PE_Percentile_in_own_5Y_history

Never compare HUL's P/E to a steel company's P/E. 
A 60x P/E for HUL might be in its own 30th percentile (cheap by HUL standards).
```

#### FMCG Red Flags

| Red Flag | Penalty |
|---|---|
| `VOLUME_DECLINE` (3 consecutive quarters) | −15 |
| `INPUT_COST_INFLATION_WITHOUT_PASSTHROUGH` | −10 |
| `PROMOTER_HOLDING_SHARP_DECLINE` (>3% in a quarter) | −15 |
| `KEY_BRAND_RECALL` or quality scandal | −20 |

